# Internal strategy / competitive collateral — never commit.
#
# docs/internal/README.md opens with "NOT for open-source distribution", but the
# whole directory was tracked anyway — competitive analysis and battle card
# included — along with two copies of the PRFAQ. The rule above only covers
# binaries, so the .md originals sailed straight through.
#
# .gitignore does nothing for files git already tracks: if one of these shows up
# staged, untrack it with `git rm --cached` rather than only adding a pattern.
#
# Patterns are matched against the real filenames these docs get written under,
# which are title-cased with spaces — not the kebab-case a pattern author reaches
# for by reflex. `launch-materials*` matched nothing: the actual file was
# "docs/AxonLLM Launch Materials.md", wrong case AND the words are mid-name, so an
# anchored lowercase prefix failed twice over. Both spellings are listed below
# because git only folds case when core.ignorecase is set (true on macOS, false on
# a Linux CI checkout), so relying on the filesystem to be forgiving is not
# portable. Leading `*` for the same reason: these names are prefixed by product.
#
# Each doc is therefore listed in BOTH spellings — spaces and hyphens — because the
# same content lands under either depending on how it was exported. Every file of
# this kind currently in the tree is a .docx caught by `*.docx` above, so these
# name rules look redundant today; they are not. The failure mode is the Markdown
# export, which is how "AxonLLM Launch Materials.md" reached the tree in the first
# place.
docs/internal/
.versions/
PRFAQ*
PR_FAQ*
*Launch Materials*
*Launch-Materials*
*launch-materials*
*Competitive*
*competitive*
*Battle Card*
*Battle-Card*
*battle-card*
*Market Analysis*
*Market-Analysis*
*Positioning*
*positioning*
# Exec/leadership briefing docs ("Intro to <person> — AxonLLM.md"). Broad on
# purpose, but note it also swallows a *public* doc named "Intro to ..." — if you
# add a getting-started page, name it "Getting Started" or negate it with `!`.
Intro to *
*Investment Business Case*
*Funding Plan*

